; 04-split-into-cases.kurt
;
; Split into cases
;
; For a proof from `A or B`, prove the same conclusion in an `A` case and a `B` case. The final
; step combines the disjunction and the two implications produced by closing the case blocks; Kurt
; prints it as `case-elim`, justified by the propositional theory's `or-elim` rule.

load prop
bool A, B, C, E

use A or B
use A implies C
use B implies C

show C
proof
    case A
        C
    case B
        C
    C
qed

; A `case` block behaves like `assume` when it closes: the first block produces `A implies C`, and
; the second produces `B implies C`. The final `C` is the explicit `case-elim` step.
;
; Any finite number of alternatives works the same way, with a `case` for each. Kurt records one
; kernel-checked `case-elim` step whose evidence includes `prop.kurt`'s general binary `or-elim`
; rule, the disjunction, and the implication produced by every case block; it does not search
; combinations of an n-premise rule.

bool G
use A or B or E
use A implies G
use B implies G
use E implies G
case A
    G
case B
    G
case E
    G
G
